---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
---
<Base
  title="Your data — LunarFlow privacy"
  description="How LunarFlow handles your data: the account it needs, cloud sync, unencrypted photo storage, non-personalised ads, and photo descriptions you switch on."
  path="/privacy"
>
  <h1 class="display text-[2.1rem] sm:text-[2.6rem]">Your data, in plain language</h1>
  <p class="mt-4 max-w-prose">
    This page names the uncomfortable parts first, on purpose. For the full legal
    text, see the <a href="/privacy-policy" class="underline">privacy policy</a>.
  </p>

  <section class="prose-body mt-14 max-w-prose">
    <span class="phase-rule text-outline dark:text-white/25" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <h2 class="display-sm mt-4 text-[1.4rem]">You need an account</h2>
    <p class="mt-2">
      LunarFlow asks for an email address and password before you can use the
      tracker. If the app cannot reach the cloud at all when it starts, you can
      continue on that device only, under a banner saying sync is unavailable —
      that's a fallback for an outage, not a normal way to use the app.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose-body mt-14 max-w-prose">
    <span class="phase-rule text-outline dark:text-white/25" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <h2 class="display-sm mt-4 text-[1.4rem]">Your logs sync to our cloud, and we can read them</h2>
    <p class="mt-2">
      Your device is the source of truth: everything is written to a database on
      your device first, and the app works fully offline. When you're signed in,
      your daily logs and preference settings also sync to our Firebase database
      so they follow you between devices.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2">
      That cloud copy is stored in plaintext. It is protected by account rules,
      not by encryption, which means the operator of the service can read it.
      It is not sold and not shared with advertisers. The on-device database
      itself is encrypted at rest with a 256-bit key generated on your device
      and held in the OS keystore — but the sync component's own working copy
      of recently-synced data is a documented exception to that encryption.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose-body mt-14 max-w-prose">
    <span class="phase-rule text-outline dark:text-white/25" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <h2 class="display-sm mt-4 text-[1.4rem]">Deletion, retention, and legal requests</h2>
    <p class="mt-2">
      Requesting account deletion wipes your device immediately and schedules the
      server copy for removal after 30 days. The automated job that carries out
      that final server-side step has not been switched on yet, so today a
      request stops sync and erases the device, but does not yet guarantee the
      server copy is erased when the 30 days elapse.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2">
      There is no retention limit beyond your account staying open. Your logs
      and any uploaded photos are kept for as long as the account exists, with
      no separate maximum on top of that.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2">
      The policy does not address law-enforcement, subpoena, or other
      government requests for your data. It has no section on what would be
      disclosed, no transparency report, and no commitment to notify you — see{' '}
      <a href="/articles/what-to-check-in-a-period-tracker-privacy-policy" class="underline">what to check in a period tracker's privacy policy</a>{' '}
      for the full checklist this page is measured against, including these
      three answers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose-body mt-14 max-w-prose">
    <span class="phase-rule text-outline dark:text-white/25" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <h2 class="display-sm mt-4 text-[1.4rem]">Ads are non-personalised, and never on the screens that matter</h2>
    <p class="mt-2">
      LunarFlow shows ads through Google AdMob. We request non-personalised ads
      only, and your health data is never sent to the ad network. Symptom
      logging, your diary and your insights carry no ads at all — those screens
      are ad-free, full stop. Buying Premium, a one-time Google Play purchase,
      removes ads everywhere else too.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose-body mt-14 max-w-prose">
    <span class="phase-rule text-outline dark:text-white/25" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <h2 class="display-sm mt-4 text-[1.4rem]">Photos and videos are a cloud-only feature, and unencrypted</h2>
    <p class="mt-2">
      The photo timeline has no on-device-only option. Adding a photo or video
      uploads it — an item only exists in LunarFlow once that upload has
      finished, so there's no way to use the timeline without your media
      leaving the device.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2">
      That upload is stored unencrypted. It's protected by your account and by
      access rules, not by encryption, and the operator of the service can
      view it. This is true for every photo and video you add, whether or not
      you ever use photo descriptions below — do not upload anything you would
      not be willing to have seen.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose-body mt-14 max-w-prose">
    <span class="phase-rule text-outline dark:text-white/25" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <h2 class="display-sm mt-4 text-[1.4rem]">Photo descriptions are off until you turn them on</h2>
    <p class="mt-2">
      LunarFlow can describe what's in a photo you choose, using Google's
      image-recognition service. It is off by default, decided separately per
      account and per device. Turning it on sends that one photo to Google when
      you tap Describe — nothing is sent automatically or in the background.
    </p>
  </section>

  <p class="mt-10 max-w-prose text-sm">
    That's the summary. The <a href="/privacy-policy" class="underline">full privacy policy</a>{' '}
    covers permissions, retention, and how to delete your data, and the{' '}
    <a href="/terms" class="underline">terms of use</a> cover purchases and warranty.
    See also what's actually <a href="/features" class="underline">in the app</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
